There are two points mentioned, the ASCII part and the separation issue.

The ASCII base of the game makes it possible to describe it in a text
file. To describe other GUI's you'd need to embed some picture formats
and it would thus result in voluminous text document formats; *that*
would, IMO, be a "monstrosity", as you call the current version.

And since the guidebook is structured, you can always skip sections.
Would it be such an advantage to separate the respective chapters into
several (two or more) files? (Maintaining *.mn, *.tex, and *.txt
file versions for each book?) And there's also the *.html version
(http://www.nethack.org/v343/Guidebook.html). Maintaining one text
version and constructing the multitude of formats is superior, IMO.

And if someone voluntarily contributes versions, extracted by topic
from the Vanilla version, and enhanced by GUI pictures as needed, no
one would be angry with him. :-)

